Schmidt and henge is a family-owned  company in Berlin for 150 years it's  been making measuring instruments for  the pharmaceutical and food industries  the company has always exported its  highly specialized products these days  half of what it produces ends up outside  the European Union including in Japan  but its business in Japan has been  stagnating CEO Matta's Kishida says  Tokyo's regulatory requirements are a  burden for his company increasing costs  and complexity in Europe we already have  to undergo a large number of  certifications it's not really clear why  we have to go through the whole thing  again it drives up our costs by 30% on  the export market the customer ends up  paying we think the standards and  certifications we have in Europe should  be recognized abroad  he says his company would be much more  competitive in Japan if there were a  free trade agreement  Atsushi Mori yama also believes in the  importance of a free trade agreement  he's in charge of a unit of the Sumitomo  corporation that manufactures special  tools for the metalworking industry his  disel Dorf based company imports tools  and parts from japan and sells them all  over europe  the unit's revenue in the EU in 2013 was  a hundred million euros business is good  but more Yama says bureaucracy takes its  toll both in time and on morale every  three years customs inspectors come  around and conduct audits that last for  weeks making sure that Sumitomo has  declared everything correctly when we  import our factory in South Germany  this is 2.7 this is 5% but if you import  in the same package from our original in  fact our original R&amp;D center then both  are 5% everything is also complicated  misleading and difficult so channel  sometimes more uyama is firmly convinced  there will be a free trade deal and he  hopes it comes soon but analysts say  there are many more rounds of  negotiations ahead before any agreement  can be signed
 

